classTweaker v1 official

# Access control for the 26.3 target.
#
# Prism's accessWidener(...) in the root build script is the only place this
# is declared; it feeds Fabric as-is and converts the same file into a NeoForge
# access transformer.
#
# The header above is the entire 1.21.11 -> 26.x difference. A 1.21.11 target is
# obfuscated and remapped by fabric-loom-remap, so it needs the "named"
# namespace. A 26.x target ships unobfuscated, where Loom expects "official" --
# which on 26.x is already the Mojang name, so the NeoForge conversion stays
# correct on both.
#
# Two format rules, both learned the hard way on 26.1:
#
#   1. The header must be line 1. Loom's ClassTweakerReaderImpl.readHeader
#      reads the first line verbatim and does not skip comments, so a file that
#      opens with a comment fails with "line 1: Invalid access widener file
#      header" even though the header is present further down. Prism's own
#      access transformer converter tolerates it, so the error only appears on
#      the Loom side and looks like a corrupt file. This file had that bug until
#      26.1's copy-up exposed it.
#   2. Separators are SPACES. A tab-separated header is rejected the same way.
#      Same split personality: Prism converts it, Loom refuses it.
#
# NO ENTRIES, and that is a real gap rather than a finished state. 26.3 has not
# been ported: it is still the Phase 1 stub, while 26.1 and 26.2 carry the
# Phase 2 content refactor.
#
# Porting 26.3 is not a copy. 26.3 deleted block codecs outright -- Block.CODEC,
# BlockBehaviour.simpleCodec(...) and Block.codec() are all gone, and
# LivingEntity.drop(ItemStack, boolean) gained a third Prediction argument -- so
# the nine block classes would each need a new definition approach, not a new
# import. That is a porting project of its own, and the replacement mechanism
# has not been identified yet.
#
# When it is ported, the entries will NOT be the ones 26.1 and 26.2 use. Those
# two carry "accessible method ... BlockEntityType <init>" for a private
# constructor; on 26.3 that constructor is public, so widening it would be a
# no-op, and the block codec work will dictate what is actually needed.
